  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PreS&E 2
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: 'Before applying to begin human trials, researchers want to confirm that
    their preclinical data will meet regulatory expectations.


    <b>The Food and
    Drug Administration (FDA) may provide guidance during preclinical research about
    what data will be needed to support future clinical trials.</b>

    

    Is
    this <b>true</b> or <b>false</b>?'
  explanation: While the FDA does not approve preclinical experiments, it can offer
    guidance to help researchers understand what safety and effectiveness data will
    be needed later to test a new treatment in people. This helps research teams
    design early studies in a way that supports future patient safety and meets regulatory
    expectations.
  correctAnswer: True
  incorrectAnswer1: False
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Safety & Ethics
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: PreClinical
